*======================================================================
* kernel.a99 - the resident kernel (SAMS pages >0A->0B, >A000->BFFF)
*
* Entered from the loader once the machine has passed its checks (SAMS of
* at least PGMIN pages, an F18A) and every bank is in SAMS.  Sets up the
* console interrupt routine's flags, the keyboard and Text 80, maps the
* boot module into slot C and runs the main loop: wait for the frame,
* poll the keyboard, hand the next key to the module's TICK.  A module
* asks to quit by setting QUITF, or to hand over to another module by
* GOMOD (module.a99).
*
* Module banks (slot C, >C000) start with an entry table:
*   >C000 INIT     called when the module is switched to
*   >C002 TICK     called every frame; R0 = next key code (high byte) or 0
*   >C004 REDRAW   by convention: the whole screen again
*   >C006 reserved
* (docs/PROGRAMMERS-GUIDE.md has the whole story.)
*======================================================================
* THE MAIN LOOP, which is the heart of the system.  After the loader
* (loader.a99) has checked the machine and set up the memory map it jumps
* here.  KSTART initialises the keyboard and the 80-column text screen, then
* the program runs as a loop of frames: wait for the video chip's frame signal
* (VSYNC), step the music, scan the keyboard, and call the running module's
* TICK with the next key.  This is why a module never loops: the loop is here,
* and a module's TICK does a slice of work and returns.  After each TICK the
* kernel looks at two flags the module may have set: QUITF (leave the program)
* and NEXTPG (switch to another module).
*
* Registers: the kernel runs in its own workspace WS (the 16 registers live in
* RAM at an address of the kernel's choosing: LWPI WS) and keeps R10 as the
* software stack pointer for .enter / .leave.  Interrupts are masked (LIMI 0)
* except for the brief moments VSYNC and .intsvc let the console's interrupt
* routine run to count frames; the console's routine does its work only at
* those moments, which is why a program that polls the keyboard in the middle
* of a video transfer can corrupt the screen.
       COPY "kdefs.a99"

       AORG >A000
* Start-up order matters: interrupts off first, our own workspace and stack
* next, then the console's interrupt flags (the console's ISR would otherwise
* move sprites and treat FCTN = as QUIT by itself), then the keyboard and the
* screen.  TMRINC is the byte the console's interrupt routine adds to its
* frame counter every frame; the kernel's VSYNC waits on that counter, so it
* must be non-zero.
KSTART LIMI 0
       LWPI WS
       LI   R10,STKTOP
       LI   R0,>5000           the console ISR: no sprite motion, no QUIT
       MOVB R0,@ISRFLG         (the application handles FCTN = itself)
       CLR  @SNDDUR            no sound list playing
       LI   R0,>0100           the console's interrupt routine adds >83FC's
       MOVB R0,@TMRINC         byte to its frame counter at >8379 each frame
       BL   @KINIT             the keyboard as at power-up
       CLR  @QUITF
       CLR  @NEXTPG
       CLR  @GOARG
       CLR  @PREVPG
       BL   @T80INI            Text 80, the font, colour scheme 0, display on
* the boot module is the first switch: from "no module" (CURCC = 0)
       LI   R0,PGBOOT
       MOV  R0,@NEXTPG
       JMP  KCHK
KMAIN  BL   @VSYNC             once per frame
       BL   @MUTICK            the song, if one is playing
       BL   @KPOLL
       BL   @KEYGET            R0 = next code or 0
       MOV  @>C002,R1          the module's TICK
       BL   *R1
* After TICK returns (or after a module's INIT) the kernel acts on the
* module's requests.  A switch asked for by GOMOD takes effect here: the old
* module's page is remembered in PREVPG (so GOMOD PREVPG goes back), the new
* module's two pages are mapped at >C000 and its INIT is called with the old
* page in R0 and the argument in R1.  The switch happens between frames, never
* in the middle of the module's own code, because the code that asked for it
* is running in the very window that is about to change.
KCHK   MOV  @QUITF,R0          a quit asked for?
       JNE  KEXIT
       MOV  @NEXTPG,R0         a switch asked for (R0 = the module's page)?
       JEQ  KMAIN
       MOV  @CURCC,@PREVPG     the module that was running
       CLR  @NEXTPG
       BL   @PAGEC             the new module into slot C
       MOV  @PREVPG,R0         INIT's arguments: the old module's page ...
       MOV  @GOARG,R1          ... and what GOMOD handed over
       MOV  @>C000,R2          its INIT
       BL   *R2
       JMP  KCHK               (INIT may itself switch or quit)

* KEXIT - quit to the TI title by the console's own reset (BLWP @>0000),
* as FCTN-QUIT does.  From Lantern's RESTRT: the sound chip silenced, the
* keys let go (at most a second), every F18A register the kernel changed put
* back as at power-up (the console's reset sets only VR0-VR7), the memory
* card's mapping switched off (pages sit in their own windows, as the
* identity map had them, so this code runs on).
KEXIT  LIMI 0
       LI   R0,>9FBF           every voice quiet
       MOVB R0,@SOUND
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@SOUND
       LI   R0,>DFFF
       MOVB R0,@SOUND
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@SOUND
       LI   R8,60              (R8: KSCAN uses R1-R4)
KX1    BL   @VSYNC             a frame
       BL   @KSCAN             the keyboard
       LI   R1,KEYMAP          all six columns up?
       LI   R2,3               3 words: 6 columns
KX2    C    *R1+,@KXFF         all up (>FF)?
       JNE  KX3
       DEC  R2                 the next
       JNE  KX2
       JMP  KX4                all let go
KX3    DEC  R8                 a frame less
       JNE  KX1                a second not yet up?
KX4    LIMI 0
       BL   @F18OFF            the F18A as at power-up
       LI   R12,AMSCRU         the memory card as at power-up: mapping off
       SBZ  1
       SBZ  0
       CLR  @CBANK0            the cartridge's bank 0 (its header: the console
*                               finds the program for its menu there; this code
*                               runs from RAM at >A000, so the switch is safe)
       BLWP @>0000             the console's own reset
KXFF   DATA >FFFF

       COPY "vdp.a99"
       COPY "f18a.a99"
       COPY "pager.a99"
       COPY "keys.a99"
       COPY "text.a99"
       COPY "video.a99"
       COPY "modules.a99"
       COPY "pool.a99"
       COPY "files.a99"
       COPY "music.a99"
       COPY "kresident.a99"
KEND
       END
